  "note": "Shared by Go validateFindingBulk (internal/app/finding_bulk_validate_test.go) and TS findingBulkPatch (web/tests/finding-bulk.test.mjs). The two readers do NOT see the same string: \"assignee\" is what an operator types into the bulk change form, and \"wire\" is what that form submits and therefore what the server measures, because findingBulkPatch stores input.assignee.trim() into the POST body. So the TS test feeds \"assignee\" and the Go test feeds \"wire\", and \"accepted\" is the single verdict both must reach: the form must not reject a name the server would store, and must not submit one the server answers with 400. The TS test also asserts wire === assignee.trim() so this column stays the real request body. The two trims agree on every ordinary space codepoint, including U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000-U+200A, U+202F, U+205F and U+3000 (both trim them) and U+200B (neither does). They disagree on exactly two, and both are covered here: U+FEFF is trimmed by JavaScript only, so its cases are the ones where \"wire\" loses a codepoint the server would have kept and stored; U+0085 is trimmed by Go only, and \"nel-u0085-control-before-go-only-trim\" pins that this never becomes reachable, because both readers reject U+0085 as a control character before any trim runs.",
